{"text": "Category K is Mild Intellectual Disability. The educational description is: A student with intellectual functioning significantly below the mean and significant limitations in at least two age-appropriate adaptive skill areas. Category K reflects a mild level of intellectual disability. This description is about educational impact and the support required for meaningful participation. It should not be used as a prediction of intelligence, personality, or future potential.\n\nOur fictional student is Grace Lee, who is in Grade 6 and uses she/her pronouns. Grace has a mild intellectual disability. She participates with Grade 6 peers and works on individualized literacy, numeracy, self-advocacy, and independence goals. This background information matters because it connects the designation to observable access needs at school.\n\nWhen we plan for Grace, we ask three practical questions: What can the student already do? What barriers are interfering with participation or learning? And what support will remove those barriers while building independence? Those questions keep the profile individualized and useful. Planning should include the student, family, teacher, case manager, and relevant specialists.\n\nI will now hand the presentation to Presenter 2, who will begin with Grace’s strengths and communication.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-K-02.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Category", "start": 0.1, "end": 0.6875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 8}, {"text": "K", "start": 0.7, "end": 1.1375, "start_char": 9, "end_char": 10}, {"text": "is", "start": 1.15, "end": 1.35, "start_char": 11, "end_char": 13}, {"text": "Mild", "start": 1.3625, "end": 1.7625, "start_char": 14, "end_char": 18}, {"text": "Intellectual", "start": 1.775, "end": 2.4875, "start_char": 19, "end_char": 31}, {"text": "Disability", "start": 2.5, "end": 3.325, "start_char": 32, "end_char": 42}, {"text": "The", "start": 3.725, "end": 3.8625, "start_char": 44, "end_char": 47}, 